      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is a clear, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nTo understand water fully, it helps to look at it through a few different lenses: its chemical makeup, its unique physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemistry of Water\nScientifically, water is a chemical compound. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* Water molecules are held together by strong covalent bonds, and water molecules stick to *each other* using hydrogen bonds. These hydrogen bonds give water many of its unique and unusual properties.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct states depending on the temperature:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form of water found in rivers, oceans, and rain.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F), though it also evaporates into the air at lower temperatures.\n\n### 3. Unique and Amazing Properties\nWater behaves very differently than most other chemicals on Earth. Some of its most important properties include:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry important nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through our bodies and through the ecosystem.\n* **Ice Floats:** When most liquids freeze, their molecules pack tightly together, making the solid denser than the liquid. Water is the opposite. When water freezes, its molecules form an open crystal structure that makes ice *less dense* than liquid water. This is why ice cubes float in a glass, and why lakes freeze from the top down, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter underneath.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion"
